WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.904
Public repositories can provide legitimate technical context through source code, commit history, issue discussions, documentation, configuration examples, and release records.

00:00:09.904 --> 00:00:17.045
Package registries and container references can reveal public dependencies, versions, publishers, and distribution relationships.

00:00:17.045 --> 00:00:23.264
Documentation may identify domains, endpoints, product names, deployment patterns, or third-party services.

00:00:23.264 --> 00:00:31.786
Example configurations can clarify architecture, but they may be outdated, generic, or unrelated to a live environment.

00:00:31.786 --> 00:00:34.780
Preserve provenance and publication dates.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:42.381
Dependency clues help defenders understand software supply-chain exposure and identify components that need internal verification.

00:00:42.381 --> 00:00:46.758
A package name alone does not prove deployment.

00:00:46.758 --> 00:00:52.977
Forks, mirrors, abandoned projects, and similarly named packages require careful identity checks.

00:00:52.977 --> 00:00:59.656
Accidental public exposure of a secret, token, or credential must be handled responsibly.

00:00:59.656 --> 00:01:03.111
Do not test or use it.

00:01:03.111 --> 00:01:14.398
Preserve only the minimum evidence needed under policy, restrict access, notify the authorized owner or approved reporting channel, and support revocation and cleanup.

00:01:14.398 --> 00:01:17.853
Repositories and registries have access rules.

00:01:17.853 --> 00:01:27.296
Public technical analysis does not authorize entry into private projects, use of leaked credentials, or retrieval of restricted artifacts.

00:01:27.296 --> 00:01:34.437
Stop at access boundaries and seek guidance when ownership or reporting authority is unclear.

00:01:34.437 --> 00:01:40.195
Report code and package findings as evidence with confidence and limitations.

00:01:40.195 --> 00:01:47.796
Connect them to internal inventories through authorized teams before asserting operational use, ownership, or vulnerability.
